GPU fix (M6D D3b-0, KNOWN_ISSUES K34): on the host render target path, a resolve read zeros from a render target
that owns the resolved tiles through EDRAM addressing wrapping. A render target owns the tiles from its base up to
its base plus the EDRAM tile count (2048), so with an estimated extent reaching past the end of the EDRAM it owns
tiles below its base: in Kingdom Under Fire: Circle of Doom, the 640-pitch 4x MSAA depth-only passes at tile 720
(scissor 8192x8192) claim the whole EDRAM, the frame's 1280x720 colour at tile 0 included, and the colour is moved
into the depth buffer by the ownership transfer. Normally the HUD's draws transfer it back before the final resolve;
a frame without them resolves tiles 0-719 from the depth buffer. Before a resolve the owning render targets are
dumped to the EDRAM buffer with dispatches whose offsets are relative to the dump area's base; the dump shader reads
the render target at (dump base + offset + tile) - render target base, so for tiles owned through wrapping the index
was negative (outside the render target: zeros, a black frame). Likewise a dump area that wraps, owned there by a
render target that does not, was read one period too far.

GetResolveCopyRectanglesToDump now builds each owner's rectangles with ResolveCopyDumpRectangle::AppendForOwnedRange,
which splits the owned range at the render target's base and gives each rectangle the period correction
(source_period_offset_tiles) that GetDispatches adds to its dispatch offsets, so the dump base plus the offset is the
tile's index in the render target's own addressing period. The EDRAM buffer is written at the same tiles as before
(the shader wraps that index); ranges that need no correction dump exactly as before. Shared by the Vulkan and
D3D12 backends (the D3D12 one is not built or tested here). Tested by tests/gpu/test_resolve_dump_wrap.cpp (the
shader's addressing for every dumped tile) and tests/render (the frames on fbo against the fsi path).

